 ## Overview
-Interpolation and Sampling Techniques provide the mechanism to reconstruct a continuous scalar field from discrete voxel samples, allowing volume rendering to evaluate density or opacity at arbitrary ray positions [S1, unofficial][S2]. This family solves the problem of discretization artifacts (aliasing, blockiness) inherent in nearest-neighbor sampling while balancing computational cost against spatial smoothness [S3]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Supersampling](3493-supersampling.md) by defining the *reconstruction kernel* (the shape of the filter) rather than the *sampling pattern* (the number of rays), and from [Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling](826-frequency-domain-volume-rendering-with-sinc-resampling.md) by operating in the spatial domain using fixed local kernels rather than global Fourier transforms [S2, S3].
+This family defines how discrete voxel data is reconstructed into a continuous scalar field and how sample positions are determined along a ray to minimize aliasing and discretization artifacts. It solves the problem of evaluating density or opacity at arbitrary ray positions that do not align with voxel grid centers, balancing computational cost against spatial smoothness [S1, unofficial][S2][S3]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Supersampling](3493-supersampling.md) by defining the *reconstruction kernel* (the shape of the filter) rather than the *sampling pattern* (the number of rays), and from [Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling](826-frequency-domain-volume-rendering-with-sinc-resampling.md) by operating in the spatial domain using fixed local kernels rather than global Fourier transforms [S2, S3].
 
