 ### Slab-based Ray Sampling
-Define each slab of thickness $2d$ by three successive samples ($sf, sm, sb$). The next slab is indexed by $(sf, sm, sb)$ with the correlation $sf = sb$ (front sample of next slab equals back sample of current slab) [S2]. Retrieve scalar values for $sf, sm, sb$ from the 3D volume [S2].
+Defines each slab of thickness $2d$ by three successive samples ($s_f, s_m, s_b$). The next slab is indexed by $(s_f, s_m, s_b)$ with the correlation $s_f = s_b$ (front sample of next slab equals back sample of current sample) [S2]. The method retrieves scalar values for $s_f, s_m, s_b$ from the 3D volume and uses a 3D texture-dependent fetch in a 3D pre-integration table to determine color and opacity values, blending successive slabs via front-to-back compositing [S2].
 
 ### Space Warping (Log+Warp)
-Warps 3D space towards the view cell center using radial distortion to bring background closer for positional encoding, dampening high frequencies in the background while preserving foreground detail [S3]. The warping function is $W(\mathbf{p}) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{|\mathbf{p}| \cdot d_{max}}}$, where $\mathbf{p}$ is the position relative to the view cell center [S3]. The warped feature vector is $\mathbf{f} = \text{encode}((\mathbf{x}(\tilde{d}_i) - \mathbf{c}) \cdot W(\mathbf{x}(\tilde{d}_i) - \mathbf{c}))$, where $\tilde{d}_i$ is the logarithmic depth [S3].
